export default [
{slug:'ipv4-cidr-subnet-worked-example',title:'Work out an IPv4 subnet from 192.0.2.143/27',category:'security',summary:'Follow the block arithmetic, then handle /31 and /32 using their explicitly different interpretations.',tools:['subnet-calculator','base-converter','uuid'],sources:['cidr','pointToPoint'],sections:[
{title:'Read the prefix as a number of network bits',paragraphs:['An IPv4 address has 32 bits. A /27 prefix assigns 27 of them to the network portion, leaving five address bits. Five bits provide 2⁵ = 32 addresses in the block. The address you enter can be any member of that block; it does not need to be the network address.','Use 192.0.2.143/27 as a worked example. The final-octet blocks begin at multiples of 32: 0, 32, 64, 96, 128 and so on. Since 143 falls between 128 and 159, the network is 192.0.2.128 and the full range ends at 192.0.2.159.']},
{title:'Separate full addresses from conventional hosts',paragraphs:['For an ordinary broadcast subnet with this prefix, the first address identifies the network and the last is the broadcast address. The conventional host range is therefore 192.0.2.129–192.0.2.158, containing 30 host addresses. The subnet mask is 255.255.255.224 and the wildcard is 0.0.0.31.','The host count is a rule under a particular interpretation, rather than an unconditional formula for every prefix. The calculator labels the interpretation beside the result so a student does not blindly subtract two from every possible block.'],table:{headers:['Item','/27 example'],rows:[['Address bits left','5'],['Total addresses','32'],['Network','192.0.2.128'],['Conventional broadcast','192.0.2.159'],['Conventional usable range','192.0.2.129–192.0.2.158']]}},
{title:'Handle the small-prefix exceptions explicitly',paragraphs:['RFC 3021 describes /31 prefixes for point-to-point links. Under that interpretation both addresses can be used as endpoints, so the calculator reports two hosts and does not assign an ordinary broadcast address. This does not claim that every device or network configuration supports that use.','A /32 describes one address, commonly a host route. Its total and host count are both one under the tool’s interpretation. These cases explain why a universal “addresses minus two” shortcut produces misleading results at the smallest blocks.']},
{title:'Use the output to understand a configuration',paragraphs:['Enter an IPv4 address and prefix, calculate, then compare network, mask and range with the exercise or configuration you are reviewing. For a /24, 32 − 24 leaves eight bits and 256 addresses. For a /27, the block is eight times smaller. Relating the prefix to block size makes it easier to spot a mistyped mask.','The tool performs local arithmetic. It does not send packets, discover devices, change routes or establish that an address is available. An address inside a mathematical host range can still be reserved or already assigned in a real network. Check the actual design, addressing records and equipment documentation before applying a configuration.']}
]},
{slug:'chmod-numeric-permissions-explained',title:'Read chmod numbers without defaulting to 777',category:'security',summary:'Build owner, group and other permissions from 4–2–1, and interpret directory search and special bits carefully.',tools:['chmod-builder','hmac-generator','password'],sources:['chmod'],sections:[
{title:'Build each digit from three permissions',paragraphs:['A basic numeric mode has one digit each for owner, group and other. Read contributes 4, write contributes 2 and execute contributes 1. Add the permissions you intend to grant for each class. Read and write give 6; read and execute give 5; read alone gives 4.','For a regular file, 644 means owner read/write, group read and other read. Its familiar symbolic form is -rw-r--r--. A 750 mode gives the owner all three permissions, the group read/execute and others none. These examples describe mode bits, rather than a recommendation for every file.']},
{title:'A directory uses execute as search',paragraphs:['For a directory, execute is commonly described as search or traversal permission. It allows looking up entries through the directory when the other relevant permissions permit the operation. Directory read, write and search interact differently from permissions on a regular document.','Selecting Directory in the builder changes the symbolic prefix and reminds you of that interpretation. A directory marked readable but not searchable can behave differently from what a beginner expects. Consult your system’s documentation and account context instead of treating a file example as a directory rule.'],list:['Choose regular file or directory.','Check the intended owner permissions.','Set group permissions for the actual group that needs access.','Set other permissions deliberately.','Read both the numeric and symbolic output before using it.']},
{title:'Leave special bits off unless you understand the requirement',paragraphs:['The optional leading digit represents special mode bits: set user ID, set group ID and sticky. Their effects depend on the operating system and object type. Symbolic output can show s or S, and t or T, depending on whether the associated execute bit is also set.','A missing lowercase x in that position does not mean the special bit vanished. For example, uppercase S can indicate that a set-ID bit is present while execute is absent. The builder makes that distinction visible so you can study it before changing anything on a real system.']},
{title:'Verify the real access problem',paragraphs:['The output is an illustrative command and uses a placeholder filename. It does not run a command or inspect your filesystem. Before applying a mode, confirm the target path, ownership, group, parent-directory permissions and any access-control lists. Those can affect access even when the final file’s three digits look correct.','Avoid broad recursive changes as a first reaction to an access error. Granting every permission to every class can create a larger problem and still fail to fix a different cause. Test a narrow, appropriate change on a file you control and verify the result with your system’s tools. Keep a record of the original mode when the change matters so it can be restored.']}
]},
{slug:'hmac-shared-key-message-authentication',title:'Generate and verify an HMAC with the right bytes',category:'security',summary:'Distinguish authentication from encryption and compare UTF-8 keys, hexadecimal bytes and exact message content.',tools:['hmac-generator','hash','text-formatter'],sources:['hmac','digest'],sections:[
{title:'Know what an HMAC can establish',paragraphs:['HMAC combines a shared secret key with message bytes and a hash algorithm to produce an authentication code. A party with the same key can recompute or verify the code. It does not hide the message. It also does not provide the public verification or identity model of a public-key digital signature.','Use the tool for learning and controlled debugging. The displayed demo key is public and provides no secrecy. Do not paste a production credential into a tool merely because it runs locally; decide whether using that credential in a browser fits your organization’s handling rules.']},
{title:'Text and hex can represent different keys',paragraphs:['With UTF-8 text encoding, the key string 6162 becomes four ASCII bytes: 36 31 36 32 in hexadecimal. With hexadecimal-byte encoding, 6162 becomes two bytes, 61 62, which represent the letters a and b in ASCII. The characters look identical in the input field, but the key material differs.','Hexadecimal input needs complete byte pairs. A single digit or a non-hex character is rejected. Choose the encoding specified by the system you are comparing against, and distinguish a key’s encoded representation from the secret bytes it represents.']},
{title:'Make the message exactly reproducible',paragraphs:['A trailing newline, different line ending or extra space changes the message bytes and therefore the HMAC. A visually similar Unicode string can also have a different byte representation. When comparing with another implementation, use a simple known test message first and specify the algorithm and encoding.'],list:['Choose HMAC SHA-256 or SHA-512 to match the reference.','Select UTF-8 text or hexadecimal key bytes.','Enter the exact message, including intended whitespace.','Generate the hexadecimal result.','Paste an expected digest into the comparison field to verify it.'],paragraphsAfter:['SHA-256 output is 32 bytes, represented by 64 hexadecimal characters. SHA-512 output is 64 bytes, represented by 128 characters. The tool validates the expected hex length and uses the browser’s native HMAC verification operation.']},
{title:'Interpret a mismatch methodically',paragraphs:['First compare algorithm, key bytes and message bytes. Then check the reference output encoding: hexadecimal, Base64 and raw bytes are different representations. Do not remove punctuation or normalize whitespace just to force a match unless the protocol explicitly requires that transformation.','HMAC is not a password-storage scheme or a malware test. A matching code proves only the relevant shared-key relationship under the assumptions of the protocol and key handling. The tool does not save the key or send the message to a processing server, but browser fields and downloaded results still deserve appropriate handling. Close the page when finished and avoid including real keys in screenshots or bug reports.']}
]},
{slug:'inspect-har-network-log-safely',title:'Inspect a HAR network log without sharing its secrets',category:'security',summary:'Read timing and size summaries, then understand what a reduced export removes and what it can still reveal.',tools:['har-viewer','url-encoder','json-formatter'],sources:['har'],sections:[
{title:'Treat the log as potentially sensitive',paragraphs:['A HAR file records browser network activity. Depending on how it was exported, it can include request URLs, headers, cookies, bodies and response information. Those fields can contain tokens, account identifiers, submitted form values or internal hostnames. A filename ending in .har does not mean the contents are safe to publish.','Chrome offers sanitized HAR export options, but sanitization has a defined scope. Even after common authentication headers and cookies are excluded, URLs, query values or bodies can still contain sensitive information. Review the actual content and the receiving party’s need before sharing a log.']},
{title:'Use a local summary to find the next question',paragraphs:['Open the HAR in Nepocore or load the synthetic sample. The viewer displays request method, URL, status, duration and recorded response bytes. It does not replay requests or automatically open the listed addresses. Sort slowest first to examine long requests, or largest first to inspect recorded payload sizes.','Filter by a URL fragment or status to narrow the table. The interface shows the first 100 matching rows, while the underlying accepted log can contain more. A response-body byte value may be missing or estimated in a HAR, so the total is a summary of recorded values, not a billing or transfer audit.']},
{title:'Do not add overlapping durations into page load time',paragraphs:['Two requests can each take 500 ms while running at the same time. Their duration sum is 1000 ms, but that does not prove the page waited one full second for them in sequence. The viewer labels its total as a sum of request durations because concurrency matters.','Use the table to identify a request worth investigating, then return to a trusted performance tool for a waterfall and the surrounding context. Status codes, redirects, caching and browser scheduling can all affect the experience. A single slow request is evidence to investigate, not a complete diagnosis.'],list:['Choose a log you are authorized to inspect.','Sort or filter to isolate a small question.','Compare the relevant URL, status, timing and byte fields.','Keep sensitive values out of screenshots and support tickets.','Export only what the recipient needs.']},
{title:'Review even a reduced export',paragraphs:['Nepocore’s reduced HAR export rebuilds an allowlisted diagnostic structure. It removes headers, cookies, bodies, original timestamps and query values, replacing query values with REDACTED. It preserves hostnames, paths, methods, status and summary measurements. Paths can still contain names, IDs or tokens.','Read the reduced file before sending it. The CSV summary also includes URLs, so it is not a secret-free alternative. The viewer accepts bounded file sizes and entry counts to limit memory use, but it cannot certify that a log contains no personal information. When reporting a bug, a synthetic reproduction with example.com addresses is often easier to share and understand than a complete production browsing session.']}
]},
{slug:'subscription-cost-monthly-yearly',title:'Compare subscription costs on the same time scale',category:'money',summary:'Normalize weekly, monthly and annual prices without confusing an average cost with the actual billing date.',tools:['subscription-tracker','budget-planner','cash-flow'],sources:['budget','cashTiming'],sections:[
{title:'Make the billing cycle explicit',paragraphs:['A list of prices is difficult to compare when one service bills weekly and another bills annually. Enter the amount charged for one cycle and select that cycle. Use one currency for the entire list; choosing a currency label does not convert foreign charges. Include taxes or fees consistently with the amounts you want to model.','The tracker annualizes weekly charges using 52 cycles, fortnightly using 26, monthly using 12, quarterly using four and yearly using one. These are planning conventions, rather than an exact schedule of every future invoice. A calendar year can contain a different number of a particular billing weekday.']},
{title:'Work through a mixed-cycle example',paragraphs:['Suppose one service costs NPR 499 monthly, a second costs NPR 2400 yearly and a third costs NPR 120 weekly. Their modeled annual costs are 5988, 2400 and 6240. The total is NPR 14,628 per year. Dividing by 12 gives an average of NPR 1219 per month.','That average does not mean your account will be charged NPR 1219 every month. The annual service can still take 2400 at once, and the weekly service can charge four or five times in a calendar month. Use the average for comparison and a cash-flow schedule for payment timing.'],table:{headers:['Charge','Annual convention'],rows:[['499 monthly','499 × 12 = 5988'],['2400 yearly','2400'],['120 weekly','120 × 52 = 6240'],['Combined average month','14,628 ÷ 12 = 1219']]}},
{title:'Review the list against actual records',paragraphs:['Use your statements or provider billing pages to check amounts and cycles. A free trial, introductory price or exchange-rate fee can make a remembered price inaccurate. Keep cancellation and renewal terms in a separate note if needed; this arithmetic tool does not contact providers or cancel anything.'],list:['Name each service without including account credentials.','Enter one-cycle price and the matching cycle.','Update totals and compare annual equivalents.','Check actual renewal dates outside the average-cost model.','Export the list before making changes.']},
{title:'Use the result as a comparison, not an automatic recommendation',paragraphs:['The lowest average cost is not necessarily the best choice. Consider whether you use the service, whether a yearly commitment is refundable and whether the actual payment date fits your circumstances. The tracker does not assess a provider or recommend a financial decision.','Optional local saving stays in this browser and is not connected to a bank or subscription account. Clear it on a shared device or export a backup when appropriate. After canceling a service through its official process, confirm the cancellation and subsequent statement yourself. Removing a row from the tracker changes only the local list.']}
]},
{slug:'debt-payoff-snowball-avalanche-model',title:'Compare debt-payoff methods under clear assumptions',category:'money',summary:'See how monthly interest, fixed payments and priority rules shape a simplified snowball or avalanche schedule.',tools:['debt-payoff','budget-planner','loan'],sources:['debt'],sections:[
{title:'Read the model before the payoff date',paragraphs:['A payoff schedule is only as meaningful as its assumptions. Nepocore uses entered balances, annual percentage rates and fixed monthly minimum amounts. Each modeled month adds interest using APR divided by 12, pays minimums where possible and applies the remaining fixed payment budget according to the chosen priority.','The model excludes fees, new borrowing, changing rates, daily interest conventions, promotional terms and lender-specific minimum formulas. Actual statements can differ. Use the output to compare arithmetic scenarios, rather than treating the final month as a lender’s payoff quote or personal financial recommendation.']},
{title:'Check a one-debt example first',paragraphs:['For a balance of 10,000 at 12% APR, the simplified monthly rate is 1%. The first month adds 100 of interest. A payment of 1000 leaves 9100 after that month. The next month’s interest is calculated on the new balance, with minor-unit rounding.','At zero APR, a 12,000 balance paid at 3000 per month takes four months under this model. These simple cases help you check that the inputs and units are correct before entering several debts. Use one currency throughout.']},
{title:'Understand the priority difference',paragraphs:['Avalanche directs extra available payment toward the highest APR among remaining debts. Snowball directs it toward the smallest remaining balance. Minimum payments are handled first. When a debt is paid off, the tool retains the full monthly payment budget and reallocates the freed amount, rather than silently reducing future payments.','A final payment can be smaller than the full budget. The tool uses integer minor units for balances and rounded monthly interest. If the entered payment budget does not reduce the aggregate modeled balance, it asks for revised inputs instead of presenting an endless payoff date.'],list:['Enter a name, current balance, APR and fixed monthly minimum for each debt.','Add the extra monthly amount you intend to model.','Run one strategy and export the schedule.','Run the other with identical inputs.','Compare total modeled interest, months and assumptions.']},
{title:'Reconcile the result with real terms',paragraphs:['A lender may calculate interest daily, change required payments or apply payments differently. Check fees, due dates, rate changes and payoff instructions directly with the lender. Do not omit an obligation because it makes the modeled schedule look better.','The tool supports a bounded number of debts and months to keep the calculation practical. It does not connect to accounts or make payments. Keep sensitive account numbers out of labels, preserve your source statements and revisit the scenario when balances or terms change. For decisions with material consequences, obtain appropriate independent guidance rather than relying on a simplified browser model alone.']}
]},
{slug:'unit-price-compare-pack-sizes-discounts',title:'Compare pack sizes using unit price and the same units',category:'money',summary:'Convert grams, kilograms, millilitres and litres before comparing a discount with a larger pack.',tools:['unit-price','percentage','unit-converter'],sources:['budget'],sections:[
{title:'Choose a comparable quantity',paragraphs:['The larger pack is not always cheaper, and the lower sticker price is not always better value. Unit price compares cost per consistent quantity. Compare mass with mass, volume with volume, length with length or count with count. A kilogram price cannot be directly compared with a litre price unless you have a valid product-specific conversion.','Enter the full pack price and the total quantity represented by that price. For a multipack of four 250 g bags, the quantity is 1000 g, rather than 250 g or four grams. Use the same currency across the comparison; the tool does not fetch exchange rates.']},
{title:'Work a discount into the net price',paragraphs:['Suppose pack A costs NPR 180 for 750 g. Its cost per gram is 180 ÷ 750 = 0.24, or NPR 240 per kilogram. Pack B costs NPR 260 for 1 kg with a 10% discount. Its net price is 260 × 0.90 = 234, so it costs NPR 234 per kilogram. Under those inputs, B is cheaper per kilogram by NPR 6.','The discount applies to the entered pack price. Include tax or other charges consistently if they belong in your comparison. A coupon with a minimum spend or a buy-one-get-one condition may need a different total price and quantity calculation before entering it.'],table:{headers:['Pack','Net price','Comparable cost'],rows:[['A: 750 g','180','240 per kg'],['B: 1 kg, 10% off 260','234','234 per kg']]}},
{title:'Keep precision until the final comparison',paragraphs:['The tool normalizes compatible units and reports the underlying comparison. Small differences can disappear when rounded to a whole currency unit too early. Compare a useful base such as 100 g or 1 kg and keep enough decimal places to understand the difference.'],list:['Check the total quantity on the package.','Choose the correct unit family.','Enter the actual price and applicable discount.','Compare both products on the same base quantity.','Verify the result against the shelf label or receipt.']},
{title:'Use price alongside practical constraints',paragraphs:['A cheaper unit price does not establish that a larger quantity suits your needs. Consider storage space, product quality and whether you can use the amount. The calculator does not assess food safety, nutrition, brand quality or whether a promotion is worthwhile for an individual household.','If products have different concentrations or serving instructions, raw mass or volume may be a poor comparison. A concentrated cleaner and a ready-to-use cleaner can require comparing equivalent usable amounts using the manufacturers’ directions. Keep those assumptions explicit. The tool handles arithmetic; the choice of a meaningful comparable quantity remains your responsibility.']}
]}
];
